{"study_accession":"PRJNA820044","secondary_study_accession":"SRP366020","title":"Exosome gene expression technology to understand human health risks in space exploration: applications for SANS","description":"Molecular profiling to characterize the effects of environmental exposures is important from the human health and performance as well as the occupational medicine perspective in space exploration. We have developed a novel exosome-based approach that allows profiling of biological processes in the body from a variety of body fluids. The technology is suitable for diagnostic applications as well as studying the pathophysiology of the Space Associated Neuro-Ocular Syndrome in astronauts and monitoring patients with chronically impaired CSF drainage or elevated intracranial pressure. In this proof-of-concept, we demonstrate that: a) exosomes from different biofluids contain a specific population of RNA transcripts; b) urine collection hardware aboard the ISS is compatible with exosome gene expression technology; c) cDNA libraries from exosomal RNA can be stored in dry form and at room temperature, representing an interesting option for the creation of longitudinal molecular catalogs that can be stored as a repository for retrospective analysis. Overall design: 1. Comparison of high ICP patient's whole blood RNA, plasma exosomal RNA, CSF and urine exosomal RNA (in tripliates) with matched post-treatment low ICP samples or healthy (low ICP) control samples. 2. Comparison of two different urine collection tubes (in triplicates). 3. Comparison of three different DNA preservatives pre and post preservation of RNASeq libraries (one replicate per preservative).","pubmed":"","geo_series":"","n_samples":46,"samples":[{"sample_accession":"SAMN26979323","study_accession":"PRJNA820044","blood_class":"plasma_serum_cfrna","class_from":"sample","title":"Healthy individual 7 urine_hybrid capture_whole exome+lincRNA enrichment_Rep 2","description":"Healthy individual 7 urine_hybr